    },
    "The raise to %@ waits for a day that doesn't hold it back. You can take it anyway." : {
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "es" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "La subida a %@ espera un día que no la detenga. Puedes tomarla de todos modos."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The range, not the exact point, is the reading." : {
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "The range, not the exact point, is the reading."
          }
        },
        "es" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "El rango, no el punto exacto, es la lectura."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The ratio compares your average load over the last ~7 days against your last ~28: what science calls ACWR (acute:chronic). 1.0 is training about your usual; 0.8 to 1.3 reads as balance (Gabbett 2016). It's a debated heuristic and does not predict injuries (Impellizzeri 2020)." : {
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "The ratio compares your average load over the last ~7 days against your last ~28: what science calls ACWR (acute:chronic). 1.0 is training about your usual; 0.8 to 1.3 reads as balance (Gabbett 2016). It's a debated heuristic and does not predict injuries (Impellizzeri 2020)."
          }
        },
        "es" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "El ratio compara tu carga media de los últimos ~7 días contra tus últimos ~28: lo que la ciencia llama ACWR (agudo:crónico). 1.0 es entrenar más o menos lo habitual; 0.8 a 1.3 se lee como equilibrio (Gabbett 2016). Es una heurística debatida y no predice lesiones (Impellizzeri 2020)."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The reading is approximate and always comes with a «probably». Temperature confirms the phase one to three days after it changes, so this looks backward, not at this moment." : {
      "localizations" : {
        "es" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "La lectura es aproximada y siempre va con un «probablemente». La temperatura confirma la fase uno a tres días después de que cambia, así que esto mira hacia atrás, no en el momento."
          }
        }
      }
    },
